---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SOCIAL } from "@/config/site-config";

const effectiveDate = { iso: "2026-10-22", label: "October 22, 2026" };

const retention = [
	{ record: "Structured feedback held by the controller", period: "30 days after the controller receives it" },
	{
		record: "Completed or rejected inbox requests (payloads and results)",
		period: "30 days from original receipt. Pending requests stay until processed or until the project is erased",
	},
	{ record: "Private generated exports", period: "7 days" },
	{ record: "Unused erasure plans", period: "7 days. A plan can be confirmed for up to 24 hours" },
	{
		record:
			"Project contracts, history, artifacts, evidence, scoped project memory, and the SPS-owned integration clone",
		period: "Until the operator erases the project",
	},
	{ record: "Diagnostic logs", period: "Until the operator erases them" },
	{ record: "Minimal hashed replay and erasure receipts", period: "For the life of the local installation" },
	{
		record: "Provider and client histories, and copies held by Discord",
		period: "Governed by those services' own controls and policies",
	},
];
---

<Layout
	title="SPS Privacy Policy"
	ogImage="images/social-preview.png"
	description="How the Self Prompting System (SPS) Discord application handles data.">
	<article>
		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">cat ~/sps/PRIVACY</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<h2><i class="icon-shield-check"></i> SPS Privacy Policy</h2>
			<p>
				This policy covers the <b>Self Prompting System</b> ("SPS") Discord application, a private engineering tool. In this
				policy, "the operator" means the person who runs SPS and is responsible for it, and "authorized users" means the people
				the operator has explicitly allowed to use its Discord interface.
			</p>
			<p>
				SPS is separate from this website. The <a href="/privacy/">website privacy policy</a> covers alexmbugua.me and its
				guestbook, and none of the guestbook's storage, spam filtering, or vendor arrangements apply to SPS.
			</p>
			<p>
				Effective <time datetime={effectiveDate.iso}>{effectiveDate.label}</time>. Read it alongside the <a
					href="/sps/terms/">SPS Terms of Service</a
				>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps status</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<h2><i class="icon-hourglass-split"></i> Where things stand</h2>
			<p>
				SPS is a Windows-first orchestration system that runs locally and carries out engineering work through Claude
				Code CLI and Codex CLI workflows, using the operator's existing personal subscription sign-ins to those tools.
				There is no separate model API backend.
			</p>
			<p>
				Its Discord interface is private and experimental. It is configured for one operator, one Discord application,
				one private server, and one channel, and it works in two stages:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><b>Status notices</b> are live. SPS posts private progress notices about project and run state.</li>
				<li>
					<b>Operator controls</b> (feedback, run control, candidate review, and export) are enabled by the operator. Until
					they are enabled, SPS does not accept feedback, control, or review requests from Discord.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps help</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<h2><i class="icon-discord"></i> What the Discord interface is for</h2>
			<p>
				It is meant only for authorized users, not for the public. It posts progress notices, and when operator controls
				are enabled, it lets authorized users:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>send feedback on authorized runs</li>
				<li>control authorized runs, such as pausing and resuming them</li>
				<li>review delivery candidates and record explicit decisions about them</li>
				<li>request private exports of SPS records</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps schema --records</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<h2><i class="icon-database"></i> What SPS records</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<b>Discord identifiers:</b> your user ID, and the server, channel, and interaction IDs for each interaction.
				</li>
				<li>
					<b>What you submit:</b> the text you enter in slash commands, including feedback. This is content you write, and
					it is recorded as submitted.
				</li>
				<li><b>Timestamps</b> for interactions and the actions they trigger.</li>
				<li>
					<b>Action dispositions and operator decisions</b>, such as whether a run was paused, or which revision of a
					candidate was accepted.
				</li>
				<li><b>References to engineering evidence</b> and audit records associated with a run.</li>
				<li>
					<b>Minimal replay and erasure receipts:</b> small hashed records that stop expired or repeated Discord events from
					restoring data that has already been erased. They are hashed, but they are not anonymous.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Keep passwords, tokens, and other people's personal information out of anything you submit. There is no
				guarantee that submitted feedback, evidence, or exports will never contain sensitive information, so the safest
				approach is not to put it there.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps schema --exclude</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<h2><i class="icon-circle-slash"></i> What it does not collect</h2>
			<p>
				SPS processes only explicit slash commands and review-button actions. It does not ingest ordinary channel
				messages or their attachments, and the Discord message-content intent is disabled, so general conversation in
				the server is outside what SPS receives.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps trace --data-flow</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<h2><i class="icon-diagram-3"></i> Where data goes</h2>
			<p>Not all of it stays on one computer:</p>
			<ul>
				<li><b>Local computer:</b> controller state is stored on the computer where SPS runs.</li>
				<li>
					<b>Model providers:</b> explicit feedback can enter the context of later tasks run through Claude Code CLI (<b
						>Anthropic</b
					>) or Codex CLI (<b>OpenAI</b>), under the operator's personal subscription sign-ins. Once sent, it is handled
					under that provider's own terms, privacy policy, and history controls.
				</li>
				<li>
					<b>Discord:</b> every interaction passes through Discord. Progress notices containing project and run state are
					posted to the configured private channel, where anyone who can view that channel can read them. Review responses,
					which carry candidate content, verification evidence, and required integration proof, are sent as ephemeral responses
					shown only to the person who asked for them.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps where --state</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<h2><i class="icon-hdd"></i> Storage</h2>
			<p>
				SPS keeps its state in a SQLite database, along with artifact and evidence files, in a protected per-user
				runtime location on the computer where SPS runs, outside the project's source repository. Windows file
				permissions limit access to that location to the current user, SYSTEM, and Administrators.
			</p>
			<p>
				The Discord bot token and private action payloads are also encrypted with Windows DPAPI. Other records,
				including artifacts, evidence, and logs, rely on those file permissions and are not separately encrypted.
			</p>
			<p>
				SPS does not make backups of its own. Copies the operator makes separately are outside SPS's retention and
				erasure procedures.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps retention --show</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<h2><i class="icon-clock-history"></i> Retention</h2>
			<table class="w-full text-sm my-4">
				<thead>
					<tr class="bg-base-200">
						<th scope="col" class="p-2 text-left text-warning border border-base-300">Record</th>
						<th scope="col" class="p-2 text-left text-warning border border-base-300">Kept for</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					{
						retention.map((row, i) => (
							<tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? "bg-base-200/50" : "bg-base-100"}>
								<td class="p-2 align-top border border-base-300">{row.record}</td>
								<td class="p-2 text-left align-top border border-base-300">{row.period}</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
			<p>
				Expiry runs during maintenance passes while the controller is running, so a record is removed on the first pass
				after it expires, not at the exact moment it expires.
			</p>
			<p>
				Erasing structured feedback removes it from future task context. Project artifacts and source code have their
				own retention scopes. Erasure removes records from SPS storage; it is not a forensic wipe of the underlying
				disk.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">mail -s "SPS request" {SOCIAL.email}</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<h2><i class="icon-envelope-at"></i> Access, correction, and deletion</h2>
			<p>
				To ask for a copy of SPS records about you, or to have them corrected or deleted, email the operator at <a
					href={`mailto:${SOCIAL.email}`}>{SOCIAL.email}</a
				>. Include your Discord user ID, the channel, and the approximate date of the interaction. Never send passwords,
				tokens, or other credentials. You don't need to open a public issue for this.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<b>Access:</b> the operator can export controller records, checked artifacts and evidence, project memory, and matching
					inbox requests into a protected local ZIP file.
				</li>
				<li>
					<b>Correction:</b> feedback is corrected by recording a new authenticated feedback event, not by editing the original.
					Project memory is corrected through a reviewed revision.
				</li>
				<li>
					<b>Deletion:</b> erasing a managed project requires stopping SPS services, an erasure plan bound by hash to the
					records it covers, and an explicit confirmation. It cancels remaining work for that project and removes managed
					controller data, project memory, the SPS-owned integration clone, associated exports, and targeted inbox payloads.
					The bot can also delete notices it posted, after checking that it owns them.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Some things have separate scopes and controls: source repositories, original external inputs, Anthropic and
				OpenAI client histories, copies the operator makes, copies held by Discord, and the minimal replay and erasure
				receipts. Removing something from SPS records may not remove it from those.
			</p>
			<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mt-4">
				<a href={`mailto:${SOCIAL.email}`} class="link-action no-underline">
					<i class="icon-envelope-at"></i>
					{SOCIAL.email}
				</a>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">ls ~/sps/third-party/</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<h2><i class="icon-link-45deg"></i> Third-party privacy information</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<a href="https://discord.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Discord Privacy Policy</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>Anthropic Privacy Policy</a
					>
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/data-usage" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>Claude Code data usage</a
					>
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://openai.com/policies/privacy-policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>OpenAI Privacy Policy</a
					>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">git log -- ~/sps/PRIVACY</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<h2><i class="icon-calendar-event"></i> Changes</h2>
			<p>
				Material changes will be reflected on this page with an updated date, and authorized users will also be notified
				when this policy changes.
			</p>
			<p>Last updated <time datetime={effectiveDate.iso}>{effectiveDate.label}</time>.</p>
		</section>

		<hr class="my-8 border-base-300" />

		<section class="mb-6">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-error font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">exit</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">_</span>
			</p>
			<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mt-4">
				<a href="/sps/terms/" class="link-action no-underline">
					<i class="icon-file-earmark-text"></i>
					SPS Terms
				</a>
				<a href="/privacy/" class="link-action no-underline">
					<i class="icon-shield-check"></i>
					Website Privacy
				</a>
			</div>
		</section>
	</article>
</Layout>
